ddiction opioid crisis  um  um you know sex work nahan talks about I  mean you know she talks about this you  know the the reasons  she's willing to talk about these very  personal  um things is to  um to destigmatize and not just to  destigmatize but you know pain would  often talk about like we need to shift  the shame from you know  towards the sacklers right that the  shame needs to be it's like misplaced  you know and Nan says that at the end of  the film that the wrong things are kept  secret you know and and I absolutely  agree and so I think what Payne was  doing is trying to like okay for the  people who need to be shamed are you  know the people who are profiting and  you know knowing that you know that  they're creating a product that's  killing people this is killed a  generation you know and it's been it's  been known like the you know the the the  way that this drug has been you know  promoted to doctors and um and abused it  was known from the early you know 2000s  I mean their journalists have been  reporting on this and then the idea that  it could continue  um and yeah so and you know know getting  back to another one of your questions in  terms of like how to approach this I  mean the anytime you make a documentary  it's also exists within sort of a  context and so the fact that there are  journalists like Patrick riding key for  Barry Meyer and other filmmakers I mean  Alex Gibney has made work and a lot of  other filmers filmmakers have made work  about the crisis which kind of then like  allows me to sort of it's it's there but  I'm assuming also that the audience is  bringing something and that that what I  felt was really unique was being able to  do a portrait of Nan and then also a  kind of Deep dive into you know activism  like what what what what is the you know  the Sparks or the that makes it  effective and  um and how can it be a bit of a  blueprint again you know Howard there's  how to survive a plague is an example of  you know you see how it happens not just  the results of a direct action right and  the change at the end is so profound I  wonder if you've what does nan think of  the film have you we did you show her  the cut before and were you worried  about that reaction well those are two  different questions I'm terrified uh  of course she's seen it I mean she's  seen it many times I mean we um we did  the interviews we did a rough cut and  then you know we we showed it with her I  mean you know the I think it's important  like this is really it's this is hard  this is hard for her  um  to to trust somebody else  um it's you know it's it concludes you  know her art her art has been about her  her life  um and and she's contributed you know at  every step of the way like and we felt  like I you know I felt the editing team  felt that you know the way that Nan  talks about her how she works with the  people she photographs that it is a  collaborative that people she lets  people tear something up if it's you  know like there is this kind of  collaborative that there was imperative  that this filmmaking also be  um in that Spirit you know which doesn't  mean I mean I'm still the director I had  the final you know Final Cut on the film  but it was collaborative and she brought  so much to you know to the film at every  stage  um so but I don't want to speak for her  so I think she's I hope she's proud  that's all I can say yeah maybe as a  general question for us to conclude  um  we look backward in your film to the  history we also look forward in terms of  how we can make a change as viewers and  participants what's more important to  you when we exit a film is taking it  with you is it like a tool something  that you can use I mean I always think  going forward I mean I you know there's  I I have you know some ambivalence about  even like storytelling like I don't want  the audience to leave with a sense of  closure and they've had some cathartic  experience that like exists within a  dark